(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-02-frequency-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 2: Frequency Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A study was conducted of bladder cancer cases in men. Cancer registries report 40 new cases of bladder cancer per 100,000 men per year. Cases were from a complete count of all patients who developed bladder cancer in several regions of the United States, and the number of men at risk was estimated from the census data in those regions. Which rate is this an example of?","choices":["Point prevalence","Incidence density","Period prevalence","Cumulative incidence","Complication rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Registries count new cases over a year in a dynamic population, where men are constantly entering and leaving the census population, so the denominator is person-time and the rate is an incidence density. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.1"},{"q":"You are reading a study of the prevalence of uterine cervix infections and want to decide if the study is scientifically sound. Which of the following is not important?","choices":["Participants are followed up for a sufficient period of time for infection to occur.","The study is done on a representative sample of the population.","All members of the population are women.","Cervical infection is clearly defined.","The study is done on a sample from a defined population."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is the item that is not important, because a prevalence study is a snapshot: it counts existing cases at a single point in time and does not follow anyone forward, so a follow-up period is not part of its design. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.2"},{"q":"A probability sample of a defined population:","choices":["Is invalidated by oversampling.","Is inferior to a random sample.","Is not representative of the population.","Results in a representative sample of the population only if there are enough people in the sample."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A random or probability sample is representative of the population sampled in the long run, so the sample must be large enough for chance variation to average out. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.3"},{"q":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis (RA) would like to know the average duration of her disease. You cannot find a study that directly answers that question. However, other studies show a prevalence of 1.5%, and annual incidence of 50/100,000. These rates have been stable. On average, how many years does the disease persist?","choices":["15","25","30","33","50"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In a steady state, prevalence equals incidence times average duration, so duration = prevalence / incidence = 0.015 / (50 / 100,000 per year) = 30 years. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.4"},{"q":"Which of the following studies is not a cohort study?","choices":["The proportion of patients with stomach cancer who survive 5 years","The risk of developing diabetes mellitus in children according to their weight","Complications of influenza vaccine among children vaccinated in 2011","The earlier course of disease in a group of patients now under care in a clinic","Patients admitted to an intensive care unit and followed up for whether they are still alive at the time of hospital discharge"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A cohort is a group with a common characteristic assembled at a similar point, such as disease onset, and followed forward; patients already under care at varied stages, whose earlier course is reviewed, share no common starting point, so this is not a cohort study. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25; 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.5"},{"q":"A sample for a study of incidence of medication errors is obtained by enrolling every 10th patient admitted to a hospital. What kind of sample is this?","choices":["Stratified sample","Probability sample","Convenience sample","Random sample","Oversample"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, as the best of these choices. Enrolling every 10th admission is a convenient rule with no stated random element or random start (strictly this is systematic sampling, which counts as a probability sample only when the starting point is chosen at random); it might produce a representative sample, but because selection is not by chance it could misrepresent the hospital's patients. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.6"},{"q":"A study was performed to determine rates of hospital-acquired infection. Participants were 1,000 patients randomly selected from 150 hospitals in 5 countries. Over a 4-month period, surveys were conducted on a single day at each hospital (the date varied by hospital), and medical records were reviewed for that date to determine the presence of hospital-acquired infection. At least one hospital-acquired infection was found in 4% of patients. Which rate is this an example of?","choices":["Point prevalence","Complication rate","Incidence density","Cumulative incidence","Period prevalence"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each patient was assessed at a single point in time (one survey day at each hospital), and the proportion with an infection on that day is point prevalence; the survey dates may differ between hospitals. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.7"},{"q":"Cohort studies of children with a first febrile seizure have shown that they have a one in three chance of having another seizure during childhood. What kind of rate is this?","choices":["Point prevalence","Cumulative incidence","Complication rate","Period prevalence","Incidence density"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The children start as a fixed-size cohort with a first seizure and are followed forward, and the proportion who develop a new event (another seizure) over time is cumulative incidence. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.8"},{"q":"Which of the following would not increase the observed incidence of disease?","choices":["More aggressive efforts to detect the disease","A true increase in incidence","A more sensitive way of detecting the disease","A lowering of the threshold for diagnosis of disease","Studying a larger sample of the population"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A larger sample only gives an estimate closer to the true incidence by reducing random error; it introduces no systematic difference in how cases are found or defined, so it would not raise the observed incidence. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.9"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man from Arizona presents to the emergency room with cough, fever, and shortness of breath for the past week. A chest radiograph is consistent with pneumonia. Among the considerations for the source of pneumonia is coccidioidomycosis, a fungus that is common in the deserts of the southwestern United States and in Mexico, but uncommon elsewhere. Which of the following best describes this infection?","choices":["Endemic","Pandemic","Incident","Epidemic","Prevalent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Endemic refers to a disease limited by geographic location, and coccidioidomycosis is found mainly in the deserts of the southwestern United States and Mexico. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 32; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.10"},{"q":"Twenty-six percent of adults report having experienced back pain lasting at least a day in the prior 3 months. Which of the following best describes this rate?","choices":["Cumulative incidence","Incidence density","Point prevalence","Complication rate","Period prevalence"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The cases existed at any time within a 3-month window, and prevalence measured over a specified time period, including anyone who had pain at any point in it, is period prevalence. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.11"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes a “dynamic population”?","choices":["It is rapidly increasing in size.","It is uniquely suited for cohort studies.","People are continually entering and leaving the population.","It is the basis for measuring cumulative incidence.","It is the best kind of population for a random sample."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A dynamic population has people continually entering (born, moving in) and leaving (dying, moving out), which is why incidence in it is measured in person-time. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.12"},{"q":"During a clinic visit, a mother wonders if her newborn could develop scoliosis (a deformity of the spine that becomes apparent after birth, most often in adolescence). To provide this estimate you review the literature. Which cohort would be most appropriate to answer her question?","choices":["Children who were referred to an orthopedic surgeon for treatment","Children born in North Carolina in 2012 and examined for scoliosis until they are adults","Children who were found to have scoliosis in a survey of children in North Carolina","Children who have scoliosis and are available for study","Children who were randomly sampled from the population of North Carolina in the spring of 2012"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A cohort is a group with a common starting characteristic followed forward, and children born in the same place and year and examined until adulthood show how often scoliosis develops over time. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.13"},{"q":"Which of the following are prevalence studies especially useful for?","choices":["Describing the incidence of disease","Studying diseases that resolve rapidly","Estimating the duration of disease","Describing the proportion of people in a defined population with the condition of interest","Establishing cause and effect"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A prevalence (cross-sectional) study assesses a defined population for a condition and reports the proportion that has it, which is why it helps plan health services. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.14"},{"q":"Last year, 800,000 Americans died of heart disease or stoke. Which of the following best describes this statistic?","choices":["Incidence density","Point prevalence","Cumulative incidence","Period prevalence","None of the above"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A rate compares events to a population over a time period, so a raw count of 800,000 deaths without a denominator is not a rate and fits none of the incidence or prevalence measures. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 2.15"}]};
